# Architecture: Project Structure

- The backend must use this top-level structure:

```text
app/
  main.py
  core/
  common/
  modules/
tests/
```

- `app/core` is reserved for application-wide setup only:

```text
app/core/
  config.py
  database.py
  security.py
  logging.py
  telemetry.py
  middleware.py
```

- `app/common` is reserved for reusable cross-module functionality only:

```text
app/common/
  exceptions/
    base.py
    handlers.py
  localization/
    service.py
    locale_resolver.py
    translations/
      en.json
      ru.json
      kk.json
  responses/
    error_response.py
    success_response.py
  pagination/
    schemas.py
    utils.py
  permissions/
    dependencies.py
```

- Every business module must live under `app/modules/<plural_business_name>` and use the same layer layout:

```text
app/modules/<module>/
  api/
    router.py
    schemas.py
    dependencies.py
  application/
    use_cases/
    dto.py
  domain/
    entities.py
    enums.py
    exceptions.py
    repositories.py
    services.py
  infrastructure/
    sqlalchemy_models.py
    sqlalchemy_repositories.py
```

- Add integration-specific infrastructure files inside the owning module, such as `jwt_token_service.py`, `bcrypt_password_hasher.py`, `firebase_push_provider.py`, or `smtp_email_provider.py`.
- Use case files must be named by the business action, such as `login_user.py`, `refresh_token.py`, `get_users.py`, `change_user_status.py`, or `send_notification.py`.
- Standard business modules should follow the same structure for `auth`, `users`, `sessions`, `notifications`, and any future plural business module.
- Tests must mirror modules and layers:

```text
tests/
  <module>/
    api/
    application/
    domain/
    infrastructure/
  conftest.py
```

- Do not place new module routers, schemas, dependencies, services, repositories, or models directly under `app/modules/<module>/`. Place them in the matching layer folder.
- Do not place module-specific business logic in `app/core` or `app/common`.
- When migrating older flat module files, preserve behavior while moving code into the target layer structure.
